//! lotus/stereo_move.rye — ALES119: stereo_move — the drag gesture carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. ALES113 named `move` — lift the `count` samples starting at `start`
//! out of the timeline and reinsert them at `dest` — for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and
//! RIGHT hold the SAME length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung carries move into stereo the way ALES117 carried cut and ALES118 carried crop: run ALES113's proven
//! mono `move` on each channel with the SAME `start`, `count`, and `dest`, so the relocation lands identically in both and the stereo image stays balanced. With cut (ALES117), crop
//! (ALES118), and now move, the stereo edit family stands whole — remove a span, keep only a span, or carry a span somewhere new, always both speakers at once.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: stereo_move(sc, start, count, dest) relocates [start, start+count) to `dest` in both channels, reusing ALES113's mono `move` per channel over its public API.
//! BOTH channels are validated BEFORE either is mutated — because move can fault only with BadRange (a span outside the samples, or a dest outside the removed timeline) and both
//! channels enter equal-length, the three range checks are made once against the shared length up front, so once they pass each mono move is pre-validated to succeed and a refusal
//! never desynchronises the channels. It reuses ALES2's EditError whole and invents none of its own.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO MOVE LAW: the left channel equals mono move(left, start, count, dest) and the right equals mono move(right, start, count, dest), each byte for byte.
//! THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit, and both hold their starting length — move relocates without changing the count of samples, so the channels stay
//! aligned. THE INVERSE LAW: stereo_move(start, count, dest) then stereo_move(dest, count, start) returns both channels to the original byte for byte. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW:
//! any refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; count 0 the identity on both, dest = start the identity, a bad range BadRange.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES119.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-020325_fill-ales119-lotus-stereo-move.md
//! Stands on: lotus/move.rye (ALES113) — the mono drag gesture run once per channel; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant;
//! lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip and the EditError reused whole; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the peak/RMS meter, used only by the witness to read the result.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It relocates an existing span within each channel through
//! the proven mono move (one bounded scratch buffer per channel), reading no byte past either channel's len — it invents no sample and changes no value it keeps. No real sample rate,
//! no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_move.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const move = @import("move.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const stereo_move_version = "20260815.020325";

/// stereo_move forwards ALES2's OWN fault reused by name — BadRange (a span outside the samples, or a dest outside the removed timeline) — and invents none of its own.
pub const StereoMoveError = timeline.EditError;

/// Relocate [start, start+count) to `dest` in both channels in lockstep, the stereo image staying balanced and each channel's length holding. Both channels are validated before either
/// is mutated: because mono move can fault only with BadRange and the channels enter equal-length, the three range checks are made once against the shared length up front, so a
/// refusal never leaves one channel moved and the other not. Reuses ALES113's mono move per channel.
pub fn stereo_move(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32, dest: u32) StereoMoveError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the (shared) current samples and `dest` lies within the timeline that remains once the span is removed, all checked without underflow and
    //            refused by name before any write — the same three guards ALES113's move keeps, run once against the common length so a refusal lands before either channel mutates.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;
    if (dest > len - count) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono move is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits.
    try move.move(&sc.left, start, count, dest);
    try move.move(&sc.right, start, count, dest);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — each relocated the same span without changing its count of samples, so the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO MOVE LAW — each channel equals ALES113's mono move on that channel byte for byte; THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW — left.len == right.len after, the length
//     held; THE INVERSE LAW — move then move(dest, count, start) returns both channels; THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — a refusal leaves both channels untouched, the identity faces
//     ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO MOVE LAW — each channel equals ALES113's mono move on that channel, proven side by side over one anchor with different per-channel samples.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try stereo_move(&sc, 0, 2, 3); // lift {0,1} and reinsert at 3, both channels

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try move.move(&ml, 0, 2, 3);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try move.move(&mr, 0, 2, 3);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // Concretely: {30,40,50,10,20} on the left, its per-channel twin on the right.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 30, 40, 50, 10, 20 })) return error.LeftSpanWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 31, 41, 51, 11, 21 })) return error.RightSpanWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW — the channels stay equal length and hold their starting length across a forward and a backward move; the stereo image stays aligned.
    {
        var fwd = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&fwd, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 });
        try stereo_move(&fwd, 1, 2, 3);
        if (fwd.left.len != fwd.right.len) return error.FwdUnbalanced;
        if (fwd.left.len != 5) return error.FwdLength;

        var back = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&back, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 });
        try stereo_move(&back, 3, 2, 0);
        if (back.left.len != back.right.len) return error.BackUnbalanced;
        if (back.left.len != 5) return error.BackLength;
        if (!eq(back.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 1, 2, 3 })) return error.BackLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(back.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 9, 10, 6, 7, 8 })) return error.BackRightWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE INVERSE LAW — stereo_move(start, count, dest) then stereo_move(dest, count, start) returns both channels to the original byte for byte.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        const l0 = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 };
        const r0 = [_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 };
        try load_stereo(&sc, &l0, &r0);
        try stereo_move(&sc, 1, 2, 3);
        try stereo_move(&sc, 3, 2, 1); // the inverse
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &l0)) return error.InverseLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &r0)) return error.InverseRightWrong;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW — a refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, proven for a span past the samples and a dest past len − count.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 });

        if (stereo_move(&sc, 2, 3, 0)) |_| return error.SpanNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (stereo_move(&sc, 0, 1, 3)) |_| return error.DestNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.RefusedWroteLeft;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 })) return error.RefusedWroteRight;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.RefusedUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count 0 the identity on both channels, dest = start the identity on both.
    {
        var zero = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&zero, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        try stereo_move(&zero, 1, 0, 2); // move nothing — the identity
        if (!eq(zero.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.ZeroLeftMoved;
        if (!eq(zero.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.ZeroRightMoved;

        var same = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&same, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        try stereo_move(&same, 1, 1, 1); // dest = start — the identity
        if (!eq(same.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.SameLeftMoved;
        if (!eq(same.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.SameRightMoved;
    }

    _ = meter;

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-move: STEREO_MOVE — the drag gesture carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. Relocate [start, start+count) to dest in both channels, reusing " ++
        "ALES113's mono move per channel; a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length, so dragging a bar carries it " ++
        "in both speakers at once. With cut (ALES117), crop (ALES118), and now move, the stereo edit family stands whole — remove a span, keep only a span, or carry a span somewhere " ++
        "new. BOTH channels are validated before either is mutated — because mono move can fault only with BadRange and the channels enter equal-length, the three range checks are " ++
        "made once against the shared length up front, so a refusal never desynchronises the channels. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO MOVE LAW: each channel equals ALES113's mono " ++
        "move on that channel byte for byte. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit, the length held, the stereo image staying aligned. THE INVERSE LAW: " ++
        "move(start, count, dest) then move(dest, count, start) returns both channels to the original. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal leaves both channels byte for byte " ++
        "untouched and still balanced, count 0 the identity on both, dest = start the identity. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips relocated through the proven mono move, reading " ++
        "no byte past either channel's len; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
